{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means keeping what serves you, with less visual clutter—not making your flat feel empty.**\n\nFor a small flat, that usually means:\n- **A complete, purposeful furniture set:** comfortable seating, a reachable table, lighting and enough storage—not lots of tiny pieces.\n- **Clear routes:** arrange furniture so doors, windows and the balcony remain easy to reach.\n- **Simple shapes and a restrained palette:** a few related colours and materials rather than many competing finishes.\n- **Mostly closed storage:** everyday belongings have a home, with a few favourite objects on display.\n- **Warmth through texture:** wood, woven fabrics and a rug can keep simple rooms from feeling stark.\n\nFor your combined living room and kitchen, I’d aim for a distinct seating zone and a clear route to the balcony. Your existing warm off-white walls could be a good starting point—minimalism doesn’t require repainting everything white.", "conversationId": "d9ef25911d3645f7b4316428dab8b3c8"}
